# Data access routing

GraphQL and the AOS bridge are two independent roads to the org, not a
pipeline. Data read over one never flows into the other.

| Operation | Route |
|---|---|
| Displaying records the user can see | **GraphQL** |
| Single-field write, no business rules | GraphQL mutation |
| Any write with business rules | **AOS bridge** |
| Multi-object write | **AOS bridge** |
| Aggregation (`COUNT`, `SUM`, `GROUP BY`) | **AOS bridge** |
| Anything needing system context | **AOS bridge** |

## Rules

- **GraphQL for reads and trivial single-record writes. Apex for anything with
  rules behind it.**
- **Never split a multi-object write across GraphQL mutations.** UI API
  mutations are single-record. Two mutations are two commits with no
  transaction boundary — a failure between them leaves partial data.
- **Send identifiers, never records.** When the user acts, send the record Id.
  Client data is untrusted, may be stale, and processors re-query anyway.
- **After any write, refetch. Never patch client state.** TDTM handlers and
  flows may have changed more than you wrote.
- **Do not reimplement processor logic in React.** The automation fires on your
  DML either way, so a hand-rolled write gets the automation but skips the
  pre-DML validation.

## Why GraphQL is safe by default

UI API always enforces the running user's object permissions, FLS and sharing.
There is no system-context mode. Apex is the surface to be careful with —
`with sharing` covers records only, not CRUD or FLS.
